  m4_a3: "Integer-percentage peaks, local voting rights: observed bars match the Monte Carlo null",
  m4_a4: "Integer-percentage peaks, RTV fee: observed bars match the Monte Carlo null",
  m5_d: "What it checks: fabricated numbers leave fingerprints in the digits — an excess of round 0s and 5s (Beber–Scacco last-digit test) and departures from Benford’s law in the second digit. Sixteen tests (two options × two digit tests × four referendums): 13 fully clean. The three borderline values (Monte Carlo p = 0.005–0.12) deviate toward an excess of the digit 4 and a deficit of 0 — the opposite of the round-number profile of invented numbers — and none survives the Bonferroni correction for 16 tests (threshold 0.003).",
  m5_c1: "RTV fee — the most-voted ballot — second digits vs Benford’s law: YES p = 0.12, NO p = 0.47 against the Monte Carlo null (2,977 and 2,900 numbers).",
  m5_c2: "Same referendum, last digits of counts ≥ 100: uniform, no excess of 0s and 5s (YES p = 0.23, NO p = 0.05). The other three referendums are analogous — see sections 6–7 of the report.",
  m5_a1: "Second-digit distribution against Benford’s law, RTV referendum: all bars within the 95% Monte Carlo band",
  m5_a2: "Last-digit distribution, RTV referendum: uniform, no excess of 0s and 5s",
  m6_d: "What it checks: two independent screens for odd precincts — each precinct against the median of its district (robust z-scores, threshold |z| > 4), and an Isolation Forest over four features (turnout, leader share, invalid ballots, size). Neighbours flag 22–40 precincts per referendum (0.7–1.3%); machine learning flags exactly 30 (1% by construction). All are tiny places (16–342 voters) with mixed directions — largely the same extremes in all four ballots (e.g. a 19-voter precinct at 100% turnout): a normal statistical tail, not a pattern. Figures show the RTV fee ballot, the most-voted one; the rest are analogous.",
  m6_c1: "RTV fee: precincts vs their district median. The 29 flagged (|z| > 4) are small rural precincts with mixed directions.",
  m6_c2: "RTV fee: Isolation Forest anomaly scores; the top 30 are size extremes such as a 16-voter premises at 81% turnout.",
  m6_a1: "Outliers versus district neighbours, RTV referendum: scattered small precincts, no cluster at high turnout",
  m6_a2: "Isolation Forest anomaly scores, RTV referendum: smooth distribution, top-25 anomalies marked",
  m7_d: "What it checks: copied protocols surface as identical result vectors (YES, NO, invalid) repeated across precincts more often than chance allows. Observed: 38–60 repeated vectors per referendum against a bootstrap expectation of 37–53 — natural collisions of similar precincts (37 of the 38 preiskava pairs lie in different districts). A cross-ballot check closes the case: the difference in YES votes between referendums equals 0 twice less often than 1 (412 vs 798 precincts) — a decay to zero, the opposite of a copy-paste spike.",
  m8_d: "What it checks: the toolkit itself. A synthetic stuffing is injected into the real data — turnout raised by 5–35 points in 10% of precincts, 60–95% of the added ballots for the leader — and every alarm fires: the Shpilkin anomalous zone grows ×12–13 (4,135–4,651 votes become 51,792–59,247). A second injection (5% of precincts rounded to a whole turnout percent) lifts the integer-peaks z only to 0.8–1.6 — an honest measurement of that test’s power at ~460 voters per precinct, as flagged above.",
